Article 3: A People's Court shall not seal up, distrain, or freeze the following property of the judgment debtor:
(1) clothing, furniture, cooking utensils, tableware, and other articles necessary for the daily life of the judgment debtor and the family members supported by him or her;
(2) necessary living expenses for the judgment debtor and the family members supported by him or her. If there is a local minimum living standard, the necessary living expenses shall be determined in accordance with that standard;
(3) articles necessary for the judgment debtor and the family members supported by him or her to complete compulsory education;
(4) undisclosed inventions or unpublished works;
(5) assistive tools and medical articles necessary for the physical disabilities of the judgment debtor and the family members supported by him or her;
(6) medals and other articles of honor or commendation obtained by the judgment debtor;
(7) property that, according to the Law of the People's Republic of China on Procedure for Concluding Treaties, is prescribed as exempt from sealing up, distraining, or freezing in treaties, agreements, and other documents of a treaty or agreement nature concluded by the People's Republic of China, the Government of the People's Republic of China, or the departments of the Government of the People's Republic of China with foreign countries or international organizations;
(8) other property that shall not be sealed up, distrained, or frozen as provided by laws or judicial interpretations.

Article 18: When sealing up, distraining, or freezing the judgment debtor's property, the enforcement personnel shall make a written record stating the following contents:
(1) the time of commencement and completion of the enforcement measures;
(2) the location, type, and quantity of the property;
(3) the keeper of the property;
(4) other matters that shall be recorded.
The enforcement personnel and the keeper shall sign the written record. Where persons provided for in Article 245 of the Civil Procedure Law are present, the persons present shall also sign the written record.

Article 28: Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall issue a ruling to release the sealing up, distraining, or freezing, and serve it on the applicant for enforcement, the judgment debtor, or a person not involved in the case:
(1) the property of a person not involved in the case has been sealed up, distrained, or frozen;
(2) the applicant for enforcement withdraws the enforcement application or waives the claim;
(3) the sealed-up, distrained, or frozen property has failed to be auctioned off or sold off, the applicant for enforcement and other enforcing creditors do not agree to accept it in satisfaction of the debt, and no other enforcement measures can be taken against the property;
(4) the debt has already been satisfied;
(5) the judgment debtor provides security and the applicant for enforcement agrees to release the sealing up, distraining, or freezing;
(6) other circumstances under which the People's Court considers that the sealing up, distraining, or freezing shall be released.
Where sealing up, distraining, or freezing implemented by way of registration is released, a notice of assistance in enforcement shall be sent to the registration authority.
